 "stimulus_html": "<p>For each of the plots below, answer the following questions:</p>\n<ol type=\"a\">\n<li>Is there a curved pattern in the data? If yes, stop here. If no, continue to part b.</li>\n<li>Classify the strength and direction of the relationship. Make a guess at the value of <span data-math=\"r\">r</span>.</li></ol>",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 531595,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "<figure>\n<img src=\"exercises/media/a67ffa85c39718a0.png\" alt=\" A scatter plot shows points arranged in decreasing order. The x-axis ranges from 30 to 30, in increments of 10. The y-axis ranges from negative 50 to 250, in increments of 50. The points are scattered in decreasing order. Some of the points are as follows: (negative 20, 150), (negative 10, 100), (0, 100), (10, 50), and (23, 0). Note: all values are approximate.\">\n</figure>",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "<span data-math=\"\\,\">\\,</span><ol type=\"a\">\n<li>No curved pattern</li>\n<li>Strong negative relationship, <span data-math=\"r \\approx  - 0.9\">r \\approx  - 0.9</span></li>\n</ol>"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>a. No, this is not curved.</p>\n<p>b. This has a fairly strong negative relationship, with a possible <i>r</i> value of −0.9.</p>\n<p>The correlation coefficient (<i>r</i>) denotes the strength and direction of a linear relationship. Positive values near 1 indicate that as one variable increases, the other does. The closer <i>r</i> is to −1, the more likely that as one variable increases, the other decreases. If <i>r</i> is zero, there is no relationship. A value of plus or minus 0.7 is considered the dividing line between strong and weak relationships. For this relationship, your guess could have been anywhere between −0.8 and −0.95. To know <i>r</i> for certain, you would need to do calculations using the coordinates of the points.</p>\n"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
